{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 singles out sky-view factor as the mechanism that dictates the nocturnal UHI and explains the cross-city gap, but the problem asks for the single mechanism that best explains why that gap is larger in some cities than in others. The target's own counterconditions concede that high winds or cloud cover can override the radiation-trapping effect, which contradicts the claim that the UHI is 'dictated by' sky view factor. More substantively, anthropogenic heat release is widely recognized as a dominant modulator of the nocturnal UHI gap across cities, and by assigning primacy to sky-view geometry the target omits the mechanism the problem is probing for.","counterexample":["Houston, a sprawling, low-rise city with a relatively high sky view factor, exhibits a nocturnal UHI comparable to or larger than denser, taller cities, driven primarily by anthropogenic heat and impervious surface fractions rather than sky-view obstruction."],"premise":"The target claims the night-time UHI is 'dictated by' urban sky-view factor and that cross-city intensity scales with built density and building height, omitting anthropogenic heat as the cross-city modulator despite the problem asking for the single best mechanism explaining the inter-city gap.","premise_evidence":[{"block":"4dd62735b908","quote":"The night-time UHI is dictated by the urban sky-view factor: deep street canyons trap longwave radiation, slowing nocturnal cooling, and cross-city intensity scales with built density and building height."},{"block":"4dd62735b908","quote":"High wind speeds mix the urban canyon air with cooler rural air, overriding the radiation-trapping effect."}],"target":"443920d25c5ea9878d46bb6088f80ea20d7fab11fa445538c600785070210df3"}]}